        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Objective of this task is to identify and characterize root-tip proteins that are differentially expressed, modified or targeted early in response to gravity stimulation. While Genetics has been fruitful at identifying genes that contribute to gravity signal transduction in the root tip of plants, it also suffers from limitations due to functional redundancy. Proteomic strategies allow direct access to proteins whose abundance, localization and/or modification vary in response to gravity stimulation, regardless of gene redundancy. When such proteins are found, reverse genetics can then be used to characterize their involvement in gravity signal transduction. This proteomic approach complements microarray analyses of gene-expression responses to gravity stimulation because they can account for translational and post-translational modifications, which constitute potential regulatory mechanisms in gravity signal transduction.
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
This proposal will describe experiments aimed at identifying more of these proteins by mass spectrometry, and investigating their distribution in root-tip cells before and after gravistimulation. Mutations will be isolated in the corresponding genes (including ARR22), and their phenotypes will be analyzed. If/when a gravitropic defect is found, we will collaborate with other investigators to analyze the effect of these mutations on amyloplast sedimentation, the visco-elastic properties of the statocytes, the gravity-induced relocalization of auxin efflux carriers in the root tip, and cytoplasmic alkalinization of the statocytes early after gravistimulation. This multidisciplinary approach will fill some of the gaps that hinder our understanding of gravity perception and signal transduction in plants. 
<P>
RESULTS:<br>
We have used a proteomic approach in Arabidopsis thaliana to uncover proteins that may play important roles in early phases of gravity signal transduction in root. Last year, we reported on the isolation and characterization of 30 proteins whose expression, modification and/or localization in root tip cells are altered within 12 min of gravity stimulation. This year, we identified 10 new differentially represented protein spots upon gravity stimulation, using a two-dimensional gel electrophoresis approach coupled with mass spectrometric identification of the corresponding proteins. This brings the total number of differentially represented protein spots to 40. We then initiated a reverse genetic analysis to better characterize the function of some of these proteins, and have been able to isolate knockout mutations in several of the corresponding genes. We also engineered Arabidopsis lines that express inverted duplications of the corresponding genes, hoping to silence both trans- and endo-genes, and generated transgenic lines that express a translational fusion between the corresponding protein and a green fluorescent polypeptide that will allow us to locate the protein within plant cells using the corresponding fluorescence. Finally, a third set of transgenic plants has been generated, which over-express several of the gravityresponding proteins. Phenotypic and cell biological analysis of these lines is underway, and should provide important clues on their role in gravity signal transduction in the root tip.
        </div>

